Then s ∈ IN if and only if the scan never fails. Every successful ri is the attained suffix
minimum just defined. The test takes O(m) integer comparisons.

Proof. The last part has an all-one refinement, so its minimum is 1. Suppose the suffix to the
right has attained minimum r. A new refinement must end at b > c for some attainable right
first part c ≥ r. Necessarily b ≥ r + 1. Conversely, a refinement ending at least r + 1 can be
attached to a suffix attaining r.
    If si ≤ r, no ending part can suffice. If si = r + 1, a sufficient ending part uses the entire
mass, forcing the singleton (si ) and minimum si . If si ≥ r + 2, the refinement (1, si − 1) is
weakly increasing and has a sufficient ending part; it attains the absolute minimum 1. These
exhaustive cases prove the induction and construct a feasible preimage whenever the scan
succeeds. Selecting a larger right first part cannot avoid failure. Lemma 3.1 completes the
equivalence.

   For example, (2, 3, 2) passes and is the image of (2, 1, 2, 1, 1), whereas (2, 2, 3) fails with
backward thresholds 1, 2. Equal total mass and equal multisets of target parts thus do not
determine image membership. The complexity assertion counts integer comparisons, not bit
operations independent of the sizes of the integers.




