Put Tk = k(k + 1)/2 for k ≥ 1, and let TN consist of the nonempty compositions of N whose
parts belong to {T1 , T2 , . . .}. This class, its count and its reset/increment representations
are known [3, 4]. We identify it with the image of F ; the test, identification and counting
consequence form one structural result.
Theorem 4.1 (Explicit image coding). There is a weight-preserving bijection Φ : IN → TN
given by the following scan and inverse parsing.
Proof. Read a successful target from right to left. Its rightmost part b ≥ 1 initializes threshold
1. From threshold r, a part r + 1 increments the threshold and a part at least r + 2 resets it to
1. Consequently, after the rightmost part, there is a unique sequence of complete reset cycles
